      "text": "In triangle ABC, AD is perpendicular to BC and AD^2 = BD * CD. Prove that triangle ABC is a right-angled triangle at A.",
      "ideal_solution": "Given: In triangle ABC, AD perp BC and AD^2 = BD * CD.\r\n=> AD / CD = BD / AD.\r\n\r\nIn right triangles ADC and BDA:\r\nAngle ADC = Angle BDA = 90 degrees.\r\nAD / CD = BD / AD (given).\r\nTherefore, triangle ADC ~ triangle BDA (by SAS similarity criterion).\r\n\r\nSince corresponding angles of similar triangles are equal:\r\nAngle C = Angle BAD   --- (1)\r\nAngle DAC = Angle B   --- (2)\r\n\r\nIn triangle ABC:\r\nAngle A + Angle B + Angle C = 180\r\nAngle A + (Angle DAC + Angle BAD) = 180 (substituting from 1 and 2)\r\nAngle A + Angle A = 180\r\n2*Angle A = 180 => Angle A = 90 degrees.\r\nTherefore, triangle ABC is right-angled at A. (Hence Proved)",
      "rubric": {
        "angle_a_90": "1.5 Marks",
        "similarity": "1.5 Marks"
      },
      "cognitive_type": null
