    {
      "question_id": "MATH_2019_AI_11",
      "chapter_id": "M10_CH06",
      "subject_code": "MATH",
      "pyq_year": 2019,
      "pyq_set": "All India Set 1",
      "paper_region": "All India",
      "marks": 3,
      "text": "Prove that the ratio of the areas of two similar triangles is equal to the square of the ratio of their corresponding sides.",
      "ideal_solution": "Given: Triangle ABC ~ Triangle PQR.\r\nTo Prove: Area(ABC) / Area(PQR) = (AB / PQ)^2 = (BC / QR)^2 = (AC / PR)^2.\r\nConstruction: Draw altitudes AM perpendicular to BC and PN perpendicular to QR.\r\n\r\nProof:\r\nArea(ABC) / Area(PQR) = [(1/2) * BC * AM] / [(1/2) * QR * PN] = (BC / QR) * (AM / PN)   --- (1)\r\n\r\nIn triangles ABM and PQN:\r\nAngle B = Angle Q (since triangle ABC ~ PQR)\r\nAngle AMB = Angle PNQ = 90 degrees\r\nTherefore, triangle ABM ~ PQN (by AA similarity criterion)\r\n=> AM / PN = AB / PQ   --- (2)\r\n\r\nSince triangle ABC ~ PQR:\r\nAB / PQ = BC / QR = AC / PR   --- (3)\r\n\r\nSubstitute (2) and (3) into (1):\r\nArea(ABC) / Area(PQR) = (BC / QR) * (BC / QR) = (BC / QR)^2.\r\nBy symmetry:\r\nArea(ABC) / Area(PQR) = (AB / PQ)^2 = (BC / QR)^2 = (AC / PR)^2. (Hence Proved)",
      "rubric": {
        "conclusion": "1 Mark",
        "construction": "1 Mark",
        "altitude_ratio": "1 Mark"
      },
      "cognitive_type": null
    },
    {
      "question_id": "MATH_2019_D_11",
      "chapter_id": "M10_CH06",
      "subject_code": "MATH",
      "pyq_year": 2019,
      "pyq_set": "Delhi Set 1",
      "paper_region": "Delhi",
      "marks": 3,
      "text": "In an equilateral triangle ABC, D is a point on side BC such that BD = 1/4 BC. Prove that 16*AD^2 = 13*AB^2.",
      "ideal_solution": "Let the side of equilateral triangle ABC be a.\r\nDraw altitude AM perpendicular to BC.\r\nAltitude of equilateral triangle bisects base: BM = a / 2.\r\nGiven: BD = a / 4.\r\nDM = BM - BD = a/2 - a/4 = a/4.\r\n\r\nIn right triangle ABM:\r\nAM^2 = AB^2 - BM^2 = a^2 - (a/2)^2 = 3a^2 / 4.\r\n\r\nIn right triangle ADM:\r\nAD^2 = AM^2 + DM^2 = 3a^2/4 + (a/4)^2 = 3a^2/4 + a^2/16\r\nAD^2 = (12a^2 + a^2) / 16 = 13a^2 / 16.\r\n16*AD^2 = 13a^2 = 13*AB^2. (Hence Proved)",
      "rubric": {
        "altitude_setup": "1.5 Marks",
        "pythagoras_proof": "1.5 Marks"
      },
      "cognitive_type": null
    },
    {
      "question_id": "MATH_2018_AI_11",
      "chapter_id": "M10_CH06",
      "subject_code": "MATH",
      "pyq_year": 2018,
      "pyq_set": "All India Set 1",
      "paper_region": "All India",
      "marks": 3,
      "text": "BL and CM are medians of a triangle ABC right angled at A. Prove that 4(BL^2 + CM^2) = 5 BC^2.",
      "ideal_solution": "In right triangle ABC right-angled at A:\r\nBC^2 = AB^2 + AC^2   --- (1)\r\n\r\nIn right triangle BAL (BL is median => AL = AC / 2):\r\nBL^2 = AB^2 + AL^2 = AB^2 + (AC/2)^2 = AB^2 + AC^2 / 4\r\nMultiply by 4:\r\n4 BL^2 = 4 AB^2 + AC^2   --- (2)\r\n\r\nIn right triangle CAM (CM is median => AM = AB / 2):\r\nCM^2 = AC^2 + AM^2 = AC^2 + (AB/2)^2 = AC^2 + AB^2 / 4\r\nMultiply by 4:\r\n4 CM^2 = 4 AC^2 + AB^2   --- (3)\r\n\r\nAdd (2) and (3):\r\n4(BL^2 + CM^2) = (4 AB^2 + AB^2) + (AC^2 + 4 AC^2)\r\n4(BL^2 + CM^2) = 5 AB^2 + 5 AC^2 = 5(AB^2 + AC^2)\r\nUsing (1):\r\n4(BL^2 + CM^2) = 5 BC^2. (Hence Proved)",
      "rubric": {
        "sum_proof": "1 Mark",
        "triangle_equations": "2 Marks"
      },
      "cognitive_type": null
    },
    {
      "question_id": "MATH_2017_AI_11",
      "chapter_id": "M10_CH06",
      "subject_code": "MATH",
      "pyq_year": 2017,
      "pyq_set": "All India Set 1",
      "paper_region": "All India",
      "marks": 3,
      "text": "In an equilateral triangle ABC, D is a point on side BC such that BD = 1/3 BC. Prove that 9*AD^2 = 7*AB^2.",
      "ideal_solution": "Let side of equilateral triangle ABC be a (AB = BC = CA = a).\r\nDraw altitude AM perpendicular to BC.\r\nIn an equilateral triangle, altitude bisects the base:\r\nBM = MC = a / 2.\r\nGiven: BD = a / 3.\r\nDM = BM - BD = a/2 - a/3 = a/6.\r\n\r\nIn right triangle ABM:\r\nAM^2 = AB^2 - BM^2 = a^2 - (a/2)^2 = a^2 - a^2/4 = 3a^2 / 4.\r\n\r\nIn right triangle ADM:\r\nAD^2 = AM^2 + DM^2\r\nAD^2 = 3a^2/4 + (a/6)^2 = 3a^2/4 + a^2/36\r\nAD^2 = (27a^2 + a^2) / 36 = 28a^2 / 36 = 7a^2 / 9\r\n9*AD^2 = 7a^2.\r\nSince AB = a:\r\n9*AD^2 = 7*AB^2. (Hence Proved)",
      "rubric": {
        "altitude_setup": "1.5 Marks",
        "pythagoras_proof": "1.5 Marks"
      },
      "cognitive_type": null
    },
    {
      "question_id": "MATH_2017_D_11",
      "chapter_id": "M10_CH06",
      "subject_code": "MATH",
      "pyq_year": 2017,
      "pyq_set": "Delhi Set 1",
      "paper_region": "Delhi",
      "marks": 3,
      "text": "Prove that if a line is drawn parallel to one side of a triangle to intersect the other two sides in distinct points, then the other two sides are divided in the same ratio.",
      "ideal_solution": "Given: In triangle ABC, line DE || BC intersects AB at D and AC at E.\r\nTo Prove: AD / DB = AE / EC.\r\nConstruction: Join BE and CD. Draw EF perpendicular to AB and DG perpendicular to AC.\r\n\r\nProof:\r\nArea(triangle ADE) = (1/2) * AD * EF\r\nArea(triangle BDE) = (1/2) * DB * EF\r\nArea(triangle ADE) / Area(triangle BDE) = AD / DB   --- (1)\r\n\r\nSimilarly:\r\nArea(triangle ADE) = (1/2) * AE * DG\r\nArea(triangle DEC) = (1/2) * EC * DG\r\nArea(triangle ADE) / Area(triangle DEC) = AE / EC   --- (2)\r\n\r\nTriangles BDE and DEC lie on the same base DE and between the same parallel lines DE and BC:\r\nArea(triangle BDE) = Area(triangle DEC)             --- (3)\r\n\r\nFrom (1), (2), and (3):\r\nAD / DB = AE / EC. (Hence Proved)",
      "rubric": {
        "conclusion": "1 Mark",
        "area_ratios": "1 Mark",
        "figure_construction": "1 Mark"
      },
      "cognitive_type": null
    }
