      "text": "The angle of elevation of the top of a tower from two points at distances of 4 m and 9 m from the base of the tower and in the same straight line with it are complementary. Prove that the height of the tower is 6 m.",
      "ideal_solution": "Let the tower be AB of height h.\r\nLet C and D be two points on the ground at distances 4 m and 9 m from the foot of the tower B:\r\nBC = 4 m, BD = 9 m.\r\nLet the angle of elevation from C be theta; then from D it is (90 - theta) since the angles are complementary.\r\n\r\nIn right triangle ABC:\r\ntan(theta) = AB / BC = h / 4   --- (1)\r\n\r\nIn right triangle ABD:\r\ntan(90 - theta) = AB / BD = h / 9\r\ncot(theta) = h / 9             --- (2)\r\n\r\nMultiply (1) and (2):\r\ntan(theta) * cot(theta) = (h / 4) * (h / 9)\r\n1 = h^2 / 36\r\nh^2 = 36\r\nh = sqrt(36) = 6 m (height cannot be negative).\r\nHence, the height of the tower is 6 m. (Proved)",
