      "text": "Prove that a parallelogram circumscribing a circle is a rhombus.",
      "ideal_solution": "Let $ABCD$ be a parallelogram circumscribing a circle, touching sides $AB, BC, CD, DA$ at $P, Q, R, S$ respectively.\r\nSince tangents from an external point to a circle are equal:\r\n$$AP = AS \\quad \\text{--- (1)}$$\r\n$$BP = BQ \\quad \\text{--- (2)}$$\r\n$$CR = CQ \\quad \\text{--- (3)}$$\r\n$$DR = DS \\quad \\text{--- (4)}$$\r\nAdding (1), (2), (3), and (4):\r\n$$(AP + BP) + (CR + DR) = (AS + DS) + (BQ + CQ)$$\r\n$$AB + CD = AD + BC \\quad \\text{--- (5)}$$\r\n\r\nSince $ABCD$ is a parallelogram, opposite sides are equal:\r\n$$AB = CD \\quad \\text{and} \\quad BC = AD$$\r\nSubstituting into (5):\r\n$$AB + AB = BC + BC$$\r\n$$2AB = 2BC \\implies AB = BC.$$\r\nSince adjacent sides are equal, parallelogram $ABCD$ is a **rhombus**. $\\blacksquare$",
