      "text": "From a point P, two tangents PA and PB are drawn to a circle with center O. If OP is equal to the diameter of the circle, prove that triangle APB is an equilateral triangle.",
      "ideal_solution": "Let radius of circle be r. Given OP = diameter = 2r.\r\nIn right triangle OAP (angle OAP = 90 degrees):\r\nsin(angle OPA) = OA / OP = r / 2r = 1/2.\r\n=> Angle OPA = 30 degrees.\r\nSimilarly, in right triangle OBP, angle OPB = 30 degrees.\r\nTotal angle APB = angle OPA + angle OPB = 30 + 30 = 60 degrees.\r\nSince PA = PB (tangents from an external point), triangle APB is isosceles with angle PAB = angle PBA.\r\nAngle PAB = (180 - 60) / 2 = 60 degrees.\r\nAll three angles are 60 degrees, so triangle APB is equilateral. (Hence Proved)",
      "rubric": {
        "trig_angle": "1 Mark",
        "equilateral_proof": "1 Mark"
      },
