      "text": "In figure, XY and X'Y' are two parallel tangents to a circle with center O and another tangent AB with point of contact C intersecting XY at A and X'Y' at B. Prove that angle AOB = 90 degrees.",
      "ideal_solution": "Join OC.\r\nIn right triangles OPA and OCA:\r\nOP = OC (radii), OA = OA (common), AP = AC (tangents from external point A).\r\nTriangle OPA congruent to Triangle OCA (SSS congruence).\r\n=> Angle POA = Angle COA = (1/2) * Angle POC   --- (1)\r\n\r\nSimilarly, Triangle OQB congruent to Triangle OCB:\r\n=> Angle QOB = Angle COB = (1/2) * Angle QOC   --- (2)\r\n\r\nSince POQ is a straight diameter line:\r\nAngle POC + Angle QOC = 180 degrees.\r\nAngle AOB = Angle COA + Angle COB = (1/2)*Angle POC + (1/2)*Angle QOC\r\nAngle AOB = (1/2) * (Angle POC + Angle QOC) = (1/2) * 180 = 90 degrees. (Hence Proved)",
