      "paper_region": "All India",
      "marks": 5,
      "text": "(a) Describe the thermite process for joining cracked railway tracks with a balanced chemical equation.\r\n(b) Explain why aluminum cannot be extracted from alumina (Al2O3) by carbon reduction. Describe the electrolytic reduction method used instead.",
      "ideal_solution": "(a) Thermite Reaction: Highly exothermic reduction of ferric oxide powder by aluminum powder:\r\nFe2O3(s) + 2Al(s) -> 2Fe(l) + Al2O3(s) + Heat.\r\nThe molten iron produced flows directly into cracks to weld railway tracks.\r\n(b) Aluminum Extraction:\r\n- Al has a higher chemical affinity for oxygen than carbon does, so carbon cannot reduce Al2O3.\r\n- Hall-H\u00e9roult Electrolytic Reduction: Purified alumina is dissolved in molten cryolite (Na3AlF6) to lower its melting point and improve conductivity. Electrolysis uses carbon (graphite) anodes and a carbon-lined cathode chamber:\r\nCathode: Al3+ + 3e- -> Al(l)\r\nAnode: C(s) + 2O2- -> CO2(g) + 4e-.",
      "rubric": {
        "affinity": "1 Mark",
        "thermite": "2 Marks",
        "electrolytic": "2 Marks"
      },
      "cognitive_type": null
