      "text": "(a) Describe the electrolytic refining of copper with a neat schematic description, specifying the anode, cathode, and electrolyte.\r\n(b) What happens to soluble and insoluble impurities during electrolytic refining?",
      "ideal_solution": "(a) Setup for Electrolytic Refining of Copper:\r\n- Anode: Slab of impure copper metal.\r\n- Cathode: Thin sheet of pure copper metal.\r\n- Electrolyte: Acidified copper sulphate solution (CuSO4 + dilute H2SO4).\r\nReaction on current passage:\r\nAt Anode: Cu(s) -> Cu2+(aq) + 2e- (impure Cu dissolves).\r\nAt Cathode: Cu2+(aq) + 2e- -> Cu(s) (pure Cu deposits).\r\n(b) Behavior of Impurities:\r\n- Soluble impurities go into the aqueous electrolyte solution.\r\n- Insoluble impurities (silver, gold, platinum) settle at the base of the tank beneath the anode as anode mud.",
