      "text": "(a) Explain the formation of calcium oxide (CaO) by electron transfer between Ca (atomic number 20) and O (atomic number 8).\r\n(b) Why do ionic compounds have high melting points?\r\n(c) Why do ionic compounds conduct electricity in the molten state but not in the solid state?",
      "ideal_solution": "(a) Electron Transfer:\r\nCa (2,8,8,2) -> Ca2+ + 2e-\r\nO (2,6) + 2e- -> O2-\r\nCa2+ + O2- -> CaO (electrostatic attraction).\r\n(b) High Melting Points: Strong electrostatic forces hold the oppositely charged ions in a rigid crystal lattice; substantial thermal energy is required to overcome these bonds.\r\n(c) Conductivity: In solid state, ions are locked into fixed positions within the crystal lattice and cannot migrate under an electric field. In the molten or aqueous state, electrostatic bonds break, allowing free ions to move toward opposite electrodes.",
