      "text": "(a) What are soaps chemically? Explain the cleaning action of soap with the help of a labeled micelle diagram.\r\n(b) Why do soaps not work effectively in hard water, whereas synthetic detergents do?",
      "ideal_solution": "(a) Soaps: Sodium or potassium salts of long-chain fatty acids (e.g., C17H35COONa).\r\nCleansing Action: Soap molecules have an ionic hydrophilic head (water-soluble) and a hydrocarbon hydrophobic tail (oil-soluble). Tails dissolve in the oil/dirt droplet while heads face outward toward water, forming spherical micelles that lift dirt into emulsion.\r\n(b) Hard Water: Contains dissolved Ca^2+ and Mg^2+ salts. Soaps react with these ions to form an insoluble white curd (scum). Detergents do not form insoluble precipitates with calcium and magnesium ions and lather freely.",
