      "text": "(a) Explain the three physiological stages of urine formation inside a human nephron.\r\n(b) Differentiate between arteries and veins based on: (i) direction of blood flow, (ii) wall thickness, (iii) presence of valves.",
      "ideal_solution": "(a) Stages:\r\n1. Glomerular Filtration: High blood pressure forces liquid plasma (water, glucose, urea, salts) across glomerulus walls into Bowman capsule lumen.\r\n2. Selective Reabsorption: Useful substances (glucose, amino acids, vital salts, water) are reabsorbed along the tubular nephron back into capillary blood.\r\n3. Tubular Secretion: Extra ions (H+, K+, creatinine) are actively discharged into collecting tubule fluid to form final urine.\r\n(b) Differences:\r\n1. Blood Flow: Arteries carry blood away from heart; veins return blood toward heart.\r\n2. Wall Thickness: Arteries possess thick, elastic muscular walls; veins have thin, less muscular walls.\r\n3. Valves: Arteries lack valves; veins contain internal pocket valves to prevent backward pooling.",
