      "text": "Why do stars twinkle in the night sky, whereas planets do not twinkle? Explain briefly.",
      "ideal_solution": "1. Stars: Being distant point sources of light, their rays pass through dynamic layers of the atmosphere with fluctuating refractive indices, causing continuous shifts in apparent position and light intensity (twinkling).\r\n2. Planets: Located much closer to Earth, planets act as extended arrays of multiple point light sources. The variations from individual points average out to zero, resulting in a steady illumination.",
