      "text": "A V-I graph for a metallic wire at two different temperatures T1 and T2 is plotted, where the slope of the curve at T1 is steeper than at T2.\r\n(a) Which of the two temperatures is higher?\r\n(b) Justify your answer using Ohm's law and resistance temperature dependency.",
      "ideal_solution": "(a) Temperature T2 is higher than T1 (assuming V is plotted on y-axis and I on x-axis: slope = V/I = R; lower slope implies lower R). Conversely, if I is on y-axis, slope = 1/R.\r\nIn standard V (y) vs I (x): Resistance R = V / I = slope.\r\nSince electrical resistance of metallic conductors increases with rising temperature, the steeper slope curve has higher resistance and corresponds to higher temperature: T1 > T2.\r\n(b) According to Ohm's law, V = IR. In pure metallic conductors, rising temperature enhances lattice ion vibrations, increasing electron collisions and electrical resistance.",
