      "text": "(a) State the Right-Hand Thumb Rule.\r\n(b) Draw the magnetic field lines pattern produced around a straight current-carrying wire.\r\n(c) What is a short circuit? Why does a circuit get overloaded? Explain the role of a safety fuse.",
      "ideal_solution": "(a) Right-Hand Thumb Rule: Hold the straight conductor in your right hand with the thumb pointing in the direction of conventional current; the curled fingers indicate the circular direction of magnetic field lines.\r\n(b) Pattern: Concentric circles centered on the wire, denser closer to the wire.\r\n(c) Short Circuit & Overloading:\r\n- Short circuit occurs when the insulation of live and neutral wires breaks, bringing them into direct contact, reducing resistance to near zero and causing a surge in current.\r\n- Overloading occurs when too many high-power appliances operate simultaneously on a single circuit.\r\n- Fuse: Contains a low melting point alloy wire that melts via Joule heating (I^2*R) during an overcurrent surge, opening the circuit to prevent fire.",
