      "text": "(a) State the principle of an electric generator.\r\n(b) Draw a schematic circuit diagram of a domestic electric circuit showing main fuse, electric meter, distribution board, live, neutral, and earth wires.\r\n(c) Why are different appliances in domestic electrical wiring always connected in parallel rather than series?",
      "ideal_solution": "(a) Principle: Electromagnetic Induction (Faraday Law). When an armature coil is rotated in a magnetic field, the flux through the coil changes, inducing an electromotive force (EMF) and current.\r\n(b) Circuit Components: Service cable -> Electricity board fuse -> Electricity meter -> Main switch (distribution box with MCBs/fuses) -> Parallel appliance lines with 3 wires: Live (220 V), Neutral (0 V), Earth.\r\n(c) Parallel Connection Advantages:\r\n1. Each appliance receives the full rated potential difference (220 V).\r\n2. Each appliance operates on an independent switch; failure or switching off of one appliance does not break the circuit for other units.\r\n3. Total equivalent resistance is reduced, allowing required current for heavy appliances.",
